# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
kant-practical-3.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: kant-practical-3.logic.r2 and kant-practical-3.logic.r2.2 have "cf_determine_will" and "cf_determine_will → cf_founded_pure_law" and "¬tc_founded_pure_law" and "tc_mere_forms"; kant-practical-3.logic.r2.3 has "tc_mere_forms" and "cf_determine_will" and "cf_founded_pure_law" and "¬tc_founded_pure_law" and "cf_determine_will ∧ cf_founded_pure_law → cf_founded_pure_will"; they differ when only "the categories of freedom refer to the determination of a free elective will", "the will they determine has as its foundation a pure practical a priori law" and "the theoretic categories are only forms of thought which designate objects in an indefinite manner by means of universal concepts of every possible intuition" are true, which the first allows
kant-practical-3.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps kant-practical-3.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the categories of freedom refer to the determination of a free elective will", "the will they determine has as its foundation a pure practical a priori law" and "the theoretic categories are only forms of thought which designate objects in an indefinite manner by means of universal concepts of every possible intuition" are true and "theoretic concepts have as their foundation a pure practical a priori law" and "the categories of freedom have as their foundation the form of a pure will, which is given in reason itself" are false. Is S1, "These categories of freedom- for so we choose to call them in contrast to those theoretic categories which are categories of physical nature- have an obvious advantage over the latter, inasmuch as the latter are only forms of thought which designate objects in an indefinite manner by means of universal concept of every possible intuition; the former, on the contrary, refer to the determination of a free elective will (to which indeed no exactly corresponding intuition can be assigned, but which has as its foundation a pure practical a priori law, which is not the case with any concepts belonging to the theoretic use of our cognitive faculties); hence, instead of the form of intuition (space and time), which does not lie in reason itself, but has to be drawn from another source, namely, the sensibility, these being elementary practical concepts have as their foundation the form of a pure will, which is given in reason and, therefore, in the thinking faculty itself.", true then?
kant-practical-3.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: kant-practical-3.logic.r2 and kant-practical-3.logic.r2.2 have "precepts_will_only" and "precepts_will_only → produce_reality" and "¬tc_produce_reality"; kant-practical-3.logic.r2.3 has "precepts_will_only" and "produce_reality" and "¬tc_produce_reality" and "precepts_will_only ∧ produce_reality → cognitions_without_intuition"; they differ when only "all precepts of pure practical reason have to do only with the determination of the will, not with the physical conditions of executing one's purpose" and "the practical a priori principles themselves produce the reality of that to which they refer (the intention of the will)" are true, which the first allows
kant-practical-3.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps kant-practical-3.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "all precepts of pure practical reason have to do only with the determination of the will, not with the physical conditions of executing one's purpose" and "the practical a priori principles themselves produce the reality of that to which they refer (the intention of the will)" are true and "theoretical concepts produce the reality of that to which they refer" and "the practical a priori principles are at once cognitions and need not wait for intuitions to acquire significance" are false. Is S2, "From this it happens that as all precepts of pure practical reason have to do only with the determination of the will, not with the physical conditions (of practical ability) of the execution of one's purpose, the practical a priori principles in relation to the supreme principle of freedom are at once cognitions, and have not to wait for intuitions in order to acquire significance, and that for this remarkable reason, because they themselves produce the reality of that to which they refer (the intention of the will), which is not the case with theoretical concepts.", true then?
kant-practical-3.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: kant-practical-3.logic.r2 and kant-practical-3.logic.r2.3 have "only_practical" and "only_practical → ordered"; kant-practical-3.logic.r2.2 has "only_practical"; they differ when only "these categories apply only to the practical reason" is true, which the second allows
kant-practical-3.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps kant-practical-3.logic.r2 and kant-practical-3.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "these categories apply only to the practical reason" is true and "the categories proceed in order from those subject to sensible conditions and morally indeterminate to those free from sensible conditions and determined merely by the moral law" is false. Is S3, "Only we must be careful to observe that these categories only apply to the practical reason; and thus they proceed in order from those which are as yet subject to sensible conditions and morally indeterminate to those which are free from sensible conditions and determined merely by the moral law.", true then?
kant-practical-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: kant-practical-3.logic.r2 has "cf_founded_pure_will ∧ tc_mere_forms → cf_advantage" and "produce_reality → cognitions_without_intuition"; kant-practical-3.logic.r2.2 has nothing from it; they differ when only "the theoretic categories are only forms of thought which designate objects in an indefinite manner by means of universal concepts of every possible intuition", "the categories of freedom have as their foundation the form of a pure will, which is given in reason itself", "the categories of freedom have an obvious advantage over the theoretic categories" and "the practical a priori principles themselves produce the reality of that to which they refer (the intention of the will)" are true, which the second allows; kant-practical-3.logic.r2.3 has "tc_mere_forms ∧ cf_founded_pure_will → cf_advantage"
kant-practical-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: kant-practical-3.logic.r2 and kant-practical-3.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if (the categories of freedom have as their foundation the form of a pure will, which is given in reason itself and the theoretic categories are only forms of thought which designate objects in an indefinite manner by means of universal concepts of every possible intuition), then the categories of freedom have an obvious advantage over the theoretic categories"; kant-practical-3.logic.r2.2 does not
kant-practical-3.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps kant-practical-3.logic.r2 and kant-practical-3.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (the categories of freedom have as their foundation the form of a pure will, which is given in reason itself and the theoretic categories are only forms of thought which designate objects in an indefinite manner by means of universal concepts of every possible intuition), then the categories of freedom have an obvious advantage over the theoretic categories"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
kant-practical-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: U2: kant-practical-3.logic.r2 and kant-practical-3.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if the practical a priori principles themselves produce the reality of that to which they refer (the intention of the will), then the practical a priori principles are at once cognitions and need not wait for intuitions to acquire significance"; kant-practical-3.logic.r2.2 does not
kant-practical-3.logic.r2: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps kant-practical-3.logic.r2 and kant-practical-3.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if the practical a priori principles themselves produce the reality of that to which they refer (the intention of the will), then the practical a priori principles are at once cognitions and need not wait for intuitions to acquire significance"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
